course: dictionary
lesson: 13456
title: Hugging: holding close and staying close
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Mum was hugging me tightly after school.» Hugging means holding someone close in your arms. The clues are «tightly» and «me»: a hug is a close, warm way to show love or comfort. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Mum was holding me close in her arms after school." It still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Hugging can also mean staying very close to something. «The road is hugging the coast all the way to town.» A road cannot put its arms round the sea, so the clue is «the coast»: the road stays right next to it. Ask yourself: does it make sense to put this meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word?
explain: A hug is a choice. In PSHE lessons you learn to ask before you hug a friend, and that everyone can say no. «Can I give you a hug?» is a kind question. If the answer is no, respect it and say, "That's okay."
fact: Hugging means holding someone close in your arms to show love, comfort or friendship.
fact: Hugging can also mean staying very close to something, like «a road hugging the coast».
fact: Hug is the basic word. Hugs, hugged and hugging show when or how often it happens: «She hugged her dog yesterday.»
fact: Phrases you may see: hugging each other, hugging tightly, hugging the coast.
fact: Before you hug someone, ask first. Everyone can say no, and that answer should always be respected.
exercise: «Grandma was hugging her grandson tightly at the station.» What does hugging mean here? | Pushing someone away with force | *Holding someone close in her arms to show love | Tickling someone until they laugh | Shouting loudly at someone
exercise: «The road is hugging the coast all the way along.» What does hugging mean here? | *Staying very close to the coast | Leaving the coast far behind | Crossing over the sea | Hiding from the coast
exercise: Which word shows that the hug happened in the past? | hugging | hugs | hug | *hugged
test: Ali says, «I can hug my friend whenever I like.» Why should Ali ask first? | Because hugs only work with grown-ups | Because hugging makes friends sad | Because hugs are against the rules everywhere | *Because everyone can say no to a hug, and asking shows respect
test: «The tired child was hugging her teddy bear in bed.» What does hugging show here? | Speed and energy | Hunger for supper | Anger at the teddy bear | *Comfort and love
exam: Which sentence uses hugging to mean staying very close to something? | *The river was hugging the path all the way round the hill | My brother is hugging his little sister on the sofa | Mum was hugging me goodbye at the gate | We were hugging each other after the match
